  "7519114996765679616": {
   "created": 1792696713,
   "author": "Nilesh J.",
   "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:groupPost:8272476-7519114995649970176",
   "text": "Training an AI model happens once. Running it never stops.\n\nThat is why AI inference, the work of answering every prompt, search and agent request, has become one of the largest markets in technology.\n\n\u2705 The AI inference market is worth USD 116.06B in 2026 and is projected to reach USD 354.25B by 2035, growing 13.2% a year. Inference now takes roughly two-thirds of all AI compute, up from about a third in 2023.\n\nThe race is changing shape. Nvidia paid USD 20B to license Groq's inference technology, Google and Amazon are scaling their own chips, and challengers like Cerebras, SambaNova and Etched raised billions this year.\n\nThe real constraints are no longer compute alone. Memory and power no",
   "disc_score": 1,
   "checks": {},
   "promoted": false,
   "miss": 0,
   "added": 1792699181
  },
  "7519268897271910400": {
   "created": 1792733406,
   "author": "Guillermo Ruiz",
   "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/gruizesteban_aicompute-cuda-acceleratedcomputing-activity-7519268897271910400-VWyt",
   "text": "This week, Google open-sourced TorchTPU and Amazon Web Services (AWS) open-sourced TorchNeuron. Two announcements and different accelerators, but similar idea: keep the developer experience familiar while changing the silicon underneath.\n\nAnd they are not alone. AMD, Intel, Modular, a Qualcomm company, Tenstorrent, Etched, MatX and others are approaching the AI compute stack from different angles.\n\nSo I tried to put the pieces together. Maybe the question is no longer \"Can I run this without CUDA?\" and we should start asking: \"What\u2019s the best piece of silicon for this workload?\"\n\n#AICompute #CUDA #AcceleratedComputing",
   "disc_score": 19,
   "checks": {},
   "promoted": false,
   "miss": 0,
   "added": 1792749820
  },
  "7519388289859043329": {
   "created": 1792761871,
   "author": "Justina Lee",
   "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/justina-lee-3a0a4a44_for-years-high-frequency-trading-firms-fought-activity-7519388289859043329-2YwP",
   "text": "For years, high-frequency trading firms fought over every nanosecond in a battle to be the fastest. \n\nNow they're fighting for compute -- building data centers and investing in AI infrastructure startups -- as they increasingly use machine learning to predict market behavior. \n\n\u201cIf you don\u2019t want to be in that game, it\u2019s going to be difficult to stay involved in the more systematic trading strategies which are required today to be a market maker,\u201d Optiver's CEO tells us. \n\nRead more in our Big Take today (link in comments) by Katherine Doherty, Dina Bass and me. (You know it's a group effort when the story quotes - *in no particular order* - HRT, Tower Research, Jump Trading, Optiver, Citade",
   "disc_score": 18,
